# Coverage floor for the whole project; read by scripts/diff-coverage.sh.
#
# It can only GO UP: if the total drops below this number the gate fails, and if it is above, the script warns so it can be raised here in the same commit. It is a minimum, not the goal: the goal is 100% of the DIFF, which is what forces testing what you touch; this floor only keeps the whole from degrading between PRs that touch no code.
# It is a number and not a list of excluded files: the only statement that does not reach the combined profile is func main(), which really runs in a subprocess whose test verifies the profile it produces (explained in cmd/gitdash/main_test.go and in the script itself), so a lower floor "to be safe" would be a lie.
# 100.00% MEASURED BOTH LOCALLY AND IN CI, with no margin needed: both give 1951/1951 statements.
#
# WHAT MADE THEM MATCH: this number used to be lower in CI than locally, and the difference (4 statements) was NOT about root, as an earlier version of this file said: GitHub's runner does not run as root (verified against the Test job log), so the 0o000 permission tests DO run there. It was lazygit: two tests carried `t.Skip("lazygit not installed")`, so on a runner without lazygit the whole path was skipped and its statements never reached the profile; they now use a fake lazygit in the PATH (withFakeLazygit), the same trick as the forge CLIs, and the profile was measured with a PATH without lazygit: 100.00%.
# ANOTHER TRAP THIS AVOIDS: the "unreadable file" cases (marker or .git that cannot be read) use EISDIR — a DIRECTORY named after the marker — and NOT 0o000, because root reads a 0o000 and a permission test would then measure one thing locally and another in CI, making the floor depend on who runs it.
# MEASURED, NOT GUESSED: CI's profile was reproduced with a PATH without lazygit, which is exactly what the runner sees.
100.00